If a patient has a low-back-pain and the low-back-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the low-back-pain.
If a patient has a neck-pain and the neck-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the neck-pain.
If a patient has a painful-musculoskeletal-injury and the painful-musculoskeletal-injury is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the painful-musculoskeletal-injury.
If a patient has a minor-surgery-pain and the minor-surgery-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the minor-surgery-pain.
If a patient has a dental-pain and the dental-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the dental-pain.
If a patient has a kidney-stone-pain and the kidney-stone-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the kidney-stone-pain.
If a patient has a headache and the headache is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the headache.
If a patient has an episodic-migraine-pain and the episodic-migraine-pain is acute then an opioid-therapy should not be first-line for the episodic-migraine-pain.
